# 208. Implement Trie (Prefix Tree)

**Problem Link:** [LeetCode – Implement Trie (Prefix Tree)](https://leetcode.com/problems/implement-trie-prefix-tree/)

## Approach: Trie (Prefix Tree)

A Trie is a tree-based data structure used to efficiently store and search strings, especially when dealing with prefixes.

Each node contains:
- `isEndOfWord`: A boolean indicating whether a complete word ends at that node.
- `children[26]`: An array of pointers representing the 26 lowercase English letters.

### Operations

1. **Insert:** Traverse each character of the word. If the corresponding child node does not exist, create a new node. Finally, mark the last node as the end of the word.

2. **Search:** Traverse the Trie character by character. If any character is missing, return `false`. Otherwise, check whether the last node marks the end of a complete word.

3. **StartsWith:** Traverse the Trie for every character in the given prefix. If all characters exist, return `true`.

## Code

```cpp
class Trie {
public:
    struct trieNode {
        bool isEndOfWord;
        trieNode* children[26];
    };

    trieNode* getNode() {
        trieNode* newNode = new trieNode();
        newNode->isEndOfWord = false;

        for(int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            newNode->children[i] = NULL;
        }

        return newNode;
    }

    trieNode* root;

    Trie() {
        root = getNode();
    }

    void insert(string word) {
        trieNode* node = root;

        for(int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {
            int idx = word[i] - 'a';

            if(node->children[idx] == NULL) {
                node->children[idx] = getNode();
            }

            node = node->children[idx];
        }

        node->isEndOfWord = true;
    }

    bool search(string word) {
        trieNode* node = root;

        for(int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {
            int idx = word[i] - 'a';

            if(node->children[idx] == NULL) {
                return false;
            }

            node = node->children[idx];
        }

        return node->isEndOfWord;
    }

    bool startsWith(string prefix) {
        trieNode* node = root;

        for(int i = 0; i < prefix.length(); i++) {
            int idx = prefix[i] - 'a';

            if(node->children[idx] == NULL) {
                return false;
            }

            node = node->children[idx];
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

Let `L` be the length of the input word or prefix.

| Operation | Time Complexity |
|---|---|
| Insert | O(L) |
| Search | O(L) |
| StartsWith | O(L) |

**Space Complexity:** O(N × 26) in the worst case, where N is the total number of nodes in the Trie. Each node stores 26 child pointers.

## Key Takeaway

Trie provides efficient insertion, exact word searching, and prefix searching by sharing common prefixes among strings. It is particularly useful in autocomplete systems, dictionaries, and word-search applications.
